module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_pathname_reresolves_its_ancestry_between_calls

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_pathname_reresolves_its_ancestry_between_calls: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_pathname_reresolves_its_ancestry_between_calls" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "a pathname re-resolves its ancestry between calls: a producer creates a directory, keeps its PATH, and addresses it again in every later operation. Each of those operations walks the name from the root afresh, so whatever the ancestry means at that instant is what it writes into. An actor able to modify any ancestor -- rename the directory and leave a symlink in its place is the cheap form -- redirects every subsequent write, and the producer returns its ordinary success value naming a path whose contents are now somebody else's. Nothing refuses, because nothing asked a second time; the check that would have asked cannot help either, since a check is itself a path resolution and the state can change between it and the use.",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-09-21 by the side-chat review of gunbc#11960 and reproduced at the filesystem level before the repair was written. gunbc.fabric_source_snapshot materialize_source_snapshot took a destination PARENT as a String, created its own child inside it with mktemp -d, and then addressed that child by name once per directory (shell.Mkdir.Parents) and once per file (Filesystem.WriteCreateNew). Reproduction: create the child, rename it aside, symlink the name at an attacker-owned directory, then run the remaining operations -- the whole snapshot lands in the attacker's tree and SnapshotMaterialized is still returned, naming the substituted path. The bytes were never in question: every object hashed to the ref that named it and the store verified all of them. CONTENT INTEGRITY AND DESTINATION INTEGRITY ARE DIFFERENT FACTS, and a content-addressed store that addresses its output by name has only the first.",

    "WHAT IS ALREADY STRUCTURAL, because it bounds the class rather than closing it. Filesystem.WriteCreateNew opens with O_CREAT|O_EXCL, which REFUSES a symlinked FINAL component (measured alongside the reproduction). So a planted link cannot redirect the FILE; only a DIRECTORY component the producer already created can be substituted. That is why the class is about ancestry and not about the leaf.",

    "THE REPAIR AT THIS SPECIMEN IS A CARRIER, NOT A CHECK. The free-form parent is deleted: ProviderWorkspace is sole-constructed behind allocate_provider_workspace, and the materializer accepts only that carrier. The attacker's precondition was write access to a parent the caller named, and there is no longer a parent a caller can name.",

    "AND DELETING THE PARAMETER DID NOT DELETE THE CHOICE, which is the second half of this specimen and the part worth carrying forward. The first repair allocated with a bare `mktemp -d`, which honours TMPDIR -- so the parent was still selectable, through the ENVIRONMENT instead of through an argument, and under a shared writable non-sticky TMPDIR a different uid can rename the workspace. The side chat reproduced that with two non-root uids, and the control under sticky /tmp refused as expected. This row claimed at that moment that the class was closed for every principal but the owner, and the claim was FALSE: it silently depended on a property of whatever directory TMPDIR happened to name. RECOGNITION RULE FOR THE GENERAL CASE: when a repair removes a parameter, ask what now supplies the value, because a default read from the environment is the same choice with no name on it.",

    "AND THE FIRST ADMISSION WAS STILL TOO WEAK, IN TWO WAYS THE SIDE CHAT REPRODUCED WITH TWO UNPRIVILEGED UIDS. It asked one question of one directory. First, A DIRECTORY'S MODE PROTECTS ITS CONTENTS AND NOT ITS OWN ENTRY: a 0700 candidate under a 0777 non-sticky GRANDPARENT is renameable by anyone who can write the grandparent, so the workspace inside it is substituted whole while its own permissions never change. Second, STICKY WITHOUT AN OWNER IS NOT A PROTECTION: sticky says an entry may be renamed by ITS OWNER, so a sticky directory owned by an untrusted principal is one that principal may still rearrange -- /tmp is safe because it is root-owned AND sticky, and the rule kept the second half and dropped the first. THE RECOGNITION RULE FOR BOTH: a permission question asked of one directory is almost never the question; the subject is the ancestry.",

    "SO THE ADMISSION IS OVER EVERY LEVEL FROM THE BASE TO THE ROOT: each must be owned by this process or by root, AND must not be writable by others unless it is sticky. The walk is by repeated `/..` under a bound that REFUSES a base deeper than the bound rather than admitting an unexamined level. TMPDIR is no longer consulted -- admitting whatever it names was the environment-shaped form of the free-form parameter this class had already removed once -- and the provider names its own candidates instead.",

    "AND THE WALK ITSELF HAD TWO HOLES, BOTH REPRODUCED, AND BOTH ARE THE SAME MISTAKE AS THE ORIGINAL ONE WEARING NEW CLOTHES. First, it answered SAFE when its level budget ran out, so an unsafe ancestor above the bound was never looked at -- and the annotation beside it claimed a deeper base refuses, so the prose asserted the wall while the code was the hole. Flipping the exhausted arm alone would refuse every walk, so the repair is a TERMINATING OBSERVATION rather than a counter: `/` is its own parent, so a level whose device and inode equal those of its own `..` IS the root; root-reached admits and budget-exhausted refuses, and the two stop sharing an answer. Second, appending `/..` walks the RESOLVED target's ancestry, so a symlinked component of the candidate is never inspected and its owner can repoint it after admission -- the side chat demonstrated exactly that with a uid-33 alias under a uid-65534 provider. The repair is to CANONICALIZE ONCE and return the canonical spelling as the admitted root, so the caller never names an alias again.",

    "WHAT THE CODE ESTABLISHES, AT EXACTLY ITS STRENGTH AND NO MORE. Given a base that was ADMITTED -- canonical, every level to the root owned by this process or root, none writable by others unless sticky -- a principal at a DIFFERENT uid cannot rename any level of that ancestry and cannot write inside the 0700 workspace. The owning uid is inside the trust boundary by assumption (operator ruling, 2026-09-21). TWO EARLIER REVISIONS OF THIS ROW OVERSTATED IT, and the pattern in both is worth more than either correction: the first said every non-owner principal was excluded FULL STOP, which was false without an ancestry walk; the second kept that sentence after the walk landed, while the walk still admitted on budget exhaustion and still never saw an alias. A SCOPE SENTENCE WRITTEN ONCE DOES NOT TRACK THE CODE UNDERNEATH IT, so each repair has to be read against the claim, not only against the defect.",

    "WHAT THIS PROTECTION MUST NEVER BE PRESENTED AS, ruled 2026-09-21 and written here because a scoped residual gets re-read later by someone who did not scope it: IT IS NOT ISOLATION BETWEEN UNTRUSTED SESSIONS SHARING A UID. The admitted parent and the 0700 workspace separate PRINCIPALS, and two sessions running as one uid are one principal to every mechanism involved -- each can rename the other's workspace, read its bytes and substitute its ancestry, and nothing here will notice. Anyone who wants two mutually untrusting workloads on one host needs separate uids, or a sandbox that is not a permission bit, and this row is not evidence that they have it. The accepted scope is exactly: an actor at a DIFFERENT uid cannot substitute, and the owning uid is inside the trust boundary by assumption.",

    "THE RESIDUAL IS HONEST AND IS NOT A WEAK CHECK. A one-time lstat or canonicalize before the writes MOVES the window rather than removing it, and citing it as coverage would be worse than not having it -- the DESIGN section 4b question of whether the check's red is authorable, answered in the direction that says do not write the check. So for an actor running as the owning uid the state is outside the modeled guarantee, declared rather than papered over.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally impossible, and the capability that reaches it is named. Directory-anchored filesystem operations reachable from .dag -- an opened directory handle plus openat/mkdirat with O_NOFOLLOW, so a path is resolved ONCE and every later operation is relative to the handle that resolution produced. Under that interface the substitution has no effect on an in-flight materialization: the handle still refers to the original directory whatever its name now means, so the producer stays bound rather than merely noticing. The state stops being writable, which is why this is a ceiling of 4 and not of 2.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, at capability grain rather than artifact grain: a host primitive that returns a directory handle and a family of operations relative to it. No primitive in this repository provides one today -- extdeps.shell and extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io are path-addressed without exception -- so this is a missing host capability and not an unwritten check, and it is NOT satisfied by adding a pre-flight lstat, by canonicalizing the path, or by re-checking the destination after the writes.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: a producer that creates a location and then keeps a STRING for it. The tell is a second operation that takes the same path -- mkdir -p here, a write there -- with nothing between them that could have noticed the name being repointed. It is easy to miss precisely because the happy path is correct and the receipt is truthful about everything except where the bytes went; the cheap discriminator is to rename the created directory aside and symlink its name elsewhere between two of the producer's operations, which is a four-line shell reproduction.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
